.TH PORELO-HOST 8 "2026-11-10" "Porelo" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
porelo-host \- the host's own program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B porelo-host
.RB [ \-\-help " | " \-\-version ]
.PP
.B porelo-host judge\-push
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B porelo-host
is run by sudo rules granting NOPASSWD access for the calling person alone,
or by root's systemd service and socket units.
Person-facing commands invoke it through the installed sudo rules;
root's maintenance commands use the grouped verbs directly.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B person purge <login>
Root's explicit purge of a recorded person whose seven-day grace period has ended.
The target is explicit, as opposed to a caller-scoped task or token operation.
Refuse root, system and unrecorded accounts, an unlocked person without an unfinished
lock, a missing or future purge deadline, any remaining membership in the person's
record, and any project-listed person or host admin in the applied organization.
Missing, malformed or incomplete organization membership refuses purge.
Take the exclusive forge.conf flock and validate the people records, subordinate-ID
files and required programs before revoking access. Finish pending locks first.
Reuse the follow's purge: revoke SSH, group, session and forge access, expel Matrix
identities, drop forge tokens and identities, stop tasks then clear the vault as the
person, disable linger, terminate sessions, stop the manager and userdel --remove.
Missing optional porelo skips only its operations. A nonzero vault clear is accepted
only for exit 1 with a valid, unrefused answer keeping transport accounts elsewhere alone.
Temporarily lifted expiry is restored and the manager stopped even on cleanup failure.
Release only this person's subordinate IDs, preserving file modes, reserve the login
in the private purged list, then remove the person record. A failure retains retry state;
an already absent account can finish cleanup. Print {"person":"<login>","purged":true}.
.TP
.B person restore\-runtime
For root at up: hold the exclusive forge.conf flock and select only recorded people
with memberships, unlocked and without a pending lock. Repair each person's private
0700 return-socket directory, missing daemon link and root-owned 0644 tmpfiles boot rule.
Preserve the person's existing daemon entry, including a dangling link. Refuse an
untrusted boot rule or a delivery directory with the wrong owner, type or mode.
Restart each admitted person's user@<uid>.service through checked systemctl.
No locked, pending or membership-free person's runtime or manager is touched.
Print {"changed":N}, counting people whose files changed, excluding manager restarts;
another call can answer zero while still restarting admitted managers.
.TP
.B judge\-push
The forge hook's socket service alone, with no SUDO_USER: read one POST /verify
HTTP/1.1 request and answer on the accepted Unix socket on standard input (fd 0);
no LISTEN_FDS is needed. Require root, take a shared flock on forge.conf and read its
account= setting, then check the forge account's numeric SO_PEERCRED uid before reading
the request. Validate the registered project and the forge-owned repository and quarantine
directories, and hold task accounts to their person's recorded porelo/<task> branches
in that project, including deletions. Refuse a task account whose person no longer exists;
porelo-admin and porelo-publisher-<project> are host accounts and retain the forge's rules,
as do person accounts. Retained ended-task records still name their branches: task end
removes the task's token.
Judge new commits through the installed porelo commit verify in a confined systemd unit
as the forge account, with the checked signers, certifiers, task certificates and KRL.
The worker has no network, writable host tree or home, and at most 270 seconds;
the parent waits at most 280 seconds and the whole request must be read within one
30-second deadline. Worker stdout is bounded by the largest serialized verdict for
10,000 commits; stderr is limited to 1 MiB.
Accept only complete worker provenance for refs the request updates, with no duplicate
commits and at most 10,000 commits. Append accepted arrival verdicts privately before
answering 200. Ledger growth does not impose a total-size limit on pushes or follow.
Signature or request
refusals answer 403 with their reasons; host failures answer 500. Only-deletion pushes
need no quarantine and record no arrival. Every HTTP answer closes the connection.
Every HTTP 500 also writes its diagnostic to the journal on standard error.
.TP
.B org status [\-\-json]
Root's read of the last applied organization, persons, projects, repositories,
and their last measured central state. Refused when SUDO_USER is set.
Takes a shared flock on /etc/porelo-host/forge.conf and contacts no network.
With \-\-json, prints one complete JSON object in the host protocol's field order.
Otherwise prints the host, organization state, person count, and project count.
.TP
.B task show <fingerprint>
Prints the complete recorded task certificate as one JSON object.
Root without SUDO_USER can read any person's task records.
Through sudo, the calling SUDO_USER must be recorded, unlocked and not revoked
by the organization. Any task whose principal task:<project>:<task> names a
project the caller belongs to is visible, using membership from the host's
people records. A task outside those projects is refused with status 65 and
exactly the same output as an unrecorded fingerprint.
Takes an exclusive flock on forge.conf.
The fingerprint must match SHA256: followed by exactly 43 base64 characters,
without padding. No certificate or token is created or changed.
.TP
.B task sweep
Root's timer only; refused with status 65 when SUDO_USER is set.
Takes an exclusive flock on /etc/porelo-host/forge.conf, retries pending person
locks, and deletes forge task tokens whose recorded certificate has ended.
Retains every task record and every token for a task whose certificate is still valid.
Prints one JSON object with a sorted swept list of login/task names.
One person's forge failure stops no other person's sweep; failures answer status
69, naming the completed deletions and failures. Removal of the temporary
administrator token is attempted before the answer, including when a request
fails; a cleanup failure refuses the call.
.TP
.B task start <task>
Through sudo only: read one Ed25519 task certificate from bounded standard input.
Require the calling person's current organization key, a single principal
 task:<project>:<task>, current validity, and membership of that project.
The installed porelo judges it again against that key and the host KRL in an
isolated systemd unit. Record the certificate privately before making the gate
 token; answer the token alone without a line end, or nothing on a forge-less host.
An identical certificate is a retry and rotates its token. Another certificate
for that task, or the same task key recorded elsewhere, is refused.
.TP
.B task end <task>
Through sudo only: delete that task's gate token alone, retain its certificate,
and print {"task":"<task>","ended":true}. An unrecorded task is refused.
.TP
.B forge issue\-token
Through sudo only: replace the person's forge tokens except lends younger than
one day, and answer the new token alone, without a line end.
Used by porelo host forge-token and the person's gathered porelo host push and
porelo host pull. The person comes from SUDO_USER, with SUDO_UID checked against
the account database; there is no person argument and stdin carries no central token.
The exact sudo rule for this existing verb is:
.nf
%porelo-people ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/porelo-host forge issue-token
.fi
The person's commands read <forge>.central from their vault, with <forge> from
root's forge.conf, and run Git in their own account. There is no root push or pull
verb. Removal uses project check-central; down is not a gathered transfer.
.TP
.B forge lend\-token
Through sudo only: make a token for one command, preserving the person's token
and every lend younger than one day. Delete older or unreadable lends.
Answer the new token alone, without a line end.
.PP
These four verbs take an exclusive flock on forge.conf for authorization,
record publication and forge changes. They take the person from SUDO_USER only.
A temporary administrator token is deleted before answering, even on failure.
No certificate or token is put on a command line or in a diagnostic.
.TP
.B org follow\-now
Through sudo only: ask the fixed porelo-host-follow.service to read the organization
now, or join its current start job, and wait for that pass. Takes no arguments or
input. SUDO_USER and SUDO_UID must identify a currently admitted, unlocked,
non-pending, unrevoked person in root's records. A task account cannot call it.
The exact sudo rule is:
.nf
%porelo-people ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/porelo-host org follow-now
.fi
The configuration lock is released before requesting or waiting for the service.
The service still runs org follow as root; no caller environment, token, login or
service name enters it. Only completion metadata is returned, never organization
status or journal contents:
.nf
{"invocation":"<32 lowercase hex digits>","completed":true,"exitCode":0}
.fi
The command returns the pass's exit status, including a failed pass. Failure to
obtain a completion prints a diagnostic on stderr with no completed answer.
The new command's account lookup is limited to ten seconds within its 660-second
wait budget; service calls take at most ten seconds each. A timeout or interrupted
wait never stops the shared pass. A private coordinator and immutable job and
invocation receipts under /run/porelo-host/follow-now retain the selected pass
through later invocations, until reboot. systemctl and busctl are checked system
programs; only the fixed unit's StartUnit job is requested, with mode fail.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help. Available to any uid with an accepted environment.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the Porelo build version. Available to any uid with an accepted environment.
.SH BOUNDARY
The environment is checked before argument parsing, including help and version.
Only the names sudo's env_reset keeps by default and those systemd sets for a
service or a socket are accepted; none of them reaches a child program, and the
program's own locale and output encoding are fixed.
The complete name allow-list is:
.nf
SUDO_USER SUDO_UID SUDO_GID SUDO_COMMAND SUDO_HOME HOME PATH
LOGNAME USER SHELL MAIL TERM LANG COLUMNS LINES INVOCATION_ID
JOURNAL_STREAM NOTIFY_SOCKET LISTEN_FDS LISTEN_PID
LISTEN_FDNAMES LISTEN_PIDFDID SO_COOKIE RUNTIME_DIRECTORY STATE_DIRECTORY
CACHE_DIRECTORY LOGS_DIRECTORY CONFIGURATION_DIRECTORY
CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY SYSTEMD_EXEC_PID MANAGERPID
WATCHDOG_PID WATCHDOG_USEC USERNAME SUDO_TTY
MEMORY_PRESSURE_WATCH MEMORY_PRESSURE_WRITE MAINPID PIDFILE
SERVICE_RESULT EXIT_CODE EXIT_STATUS TRIGGER_UNIT
TRIGGER_PATH TRIGGER_TIMER_REALTIME_USEC
TRIGGER_TIMER_MONOTONIC_USEC MONITOR_SERVICE_RESULT
MONITOR_EXIT_CODE MONITOR_EXIT_STATUS MONITOR_INVOCATION_ID
MONITOR_UNIT REMOTE_ADDR REMOTE_PORT LANGUAGE LINGUAS
_XKB_CHARSET TZ COLORS COLORTERM LS_COLORS DISPLAY XAUTHORITY
XAUTHORIZATION XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP HOSTNAME KRB5CCNAME PS1 PS2
LC_*
.fi
Unknown names are refused with the variable's name, never its value:
a widened sudo env_keep is a broken host configuration.
Accepted variables do not configure behavior; SUDO_USER and SUDO_UID identify
the person; LISTEN_FDS and LISTEN_PID authenticate the accepted socket handoff.
HOME, PATH and directory variables are never used to locate configuration.
.PP
The person for a caller-scoped task or token verb is taken only from SUDO_USER,
never from an argument. Root's person purge instead names its maintenance target.
The login must be a non-root account and SUDO_UID must match the system account database.
Missing, empty, invalid, unknown or mismatching identities are refused.
Root's unit invocations need no person until a verb acts for one.
.PP
Other invocations require real uid 0, except forge gate and forge measure.
Every non-informational invocation still requires the host's own user namespace.
Argument files are not expanded and JVM runtime options are ordinary arguments.
Child programs are checked through SystemProgram, run without a shell from /,
with a fixed locale: LANG=C.UTF-8 for task work, LC_ALL=C for project work.
Children use PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin; nothing under /usr/local is run.
Command output is bounded. Person account lookup has no deadline; maintenance
commands take at most 60 seconds. Project transfers keep their own deadlines.
Root-owned data files must be regular files without symbolic links;
the file and every parent through / must be root-owned and not writable
by group or others, including sticky temporary directories.
Reads are bounded.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 64 (EX_USAGE)
Invalid arguments, no verb, or a task start/end or forge token call without SUDO_USER.
.TP
.B 65 (EX_DATAERR)
Invalid host records or project data, a root-only verb called through sudo,
no visible task, a refused task/token caller, or a refused forge operation.
.TP
.B 69 (EX_UNAVAILABLE)
A required dependency or account database could not answer.
Person maintenance, task and forge token verbs also return this status when not root, as do project
verbs other than check-central. Forge gate and measure can run without root.
.TP
.B 73 (EX_CANTCREAT)
A task path is unsafe, or the host's files or configuration lock could not be read.
.TP
.B 77 (EX_NOPERM)
Root uid, the host user namespace or a valid sudo person could not be established.
.TP
.B 78 (EX_CONFIG)
The invocation environment contains an unapproved variable.
.SS Codes by verb
Shared boundary codes: 77 no valid root or sudo person; 78 unapproved environment variable.
Pool verbs require root (77); forge gate and measure still check the host namespace (77).
.TP
.B person purge
0 person purged; 64 bad arguments or login; 65 unknown/system account, unlocked or not-due
person, retained membership, or invalid/incomplete organization or people records;
69 not root, unsafe/unreadable host files, dependency, account, Matrix, forge or cleanup failure;
73 unsafe delivery/key/boot-rule path, lock, publication or unhandled file failure.
.TP
.B person restore\-runtime
0 runtime files repaired and admitted managers restarted; 64 bad arguments;
65 malformed people records; 69 not root, unsafe/unreadable host files, missing or unusable
account, dependency or manager failure; 73 unsafe runtime/boot-rule path, lock or file failure.
.TP
.B judge\-push
0 one hook request answered, including HTTP refusals and host failures;
64 bad arguments; 65 SUDO_USER; 69 not root; 73 no accepted Unix socket or activation failure.
Within an accepted connection, request and data refusals use HTTP 403,
and dependency, path, verifier or recording failures use HTTP 500.
Boundary codes 77 and 78 still apply before the verb runs.
.TP
.B org status
0 status shown; 64 bad arguments; 65 sudo or invalid records;
69 unsafe or unreadable host files; 73 lock or unhandled file/data failure.
.TP
.B org follow
0 followed or read key not yet added; 64 bad arguments; 65 sudo, invalid pin, records or signed state;
69 host file, dependency, deadline or reconciliation failure; 73 unsafe path, lock, publication or unhandled file/data failure.
.TP
.B org refresh
0 refreshed; 64 bad arguments; 65 sudo, invalid or unexpected token, pin, records or signed state;
69 host file, dependency, deadline or reconciliation failure; 73 unsafe path, input, lock, publication or unhandled file/data failure.
.TP
.B org follow\-now
0 completed pass; a completed failure returns that pass's nonzero exit status and
the same completed JSON answer. Without a completion: 64 bad arguments or no
SUDO_USER; 65 invalid sudo identity or person not admitted; 69 unavailable service,
account lookup, receipt, timeout or interrupted wait; 73 unsafe coordination path,
configuration lock or receipt publication failure. Shared boundary codes still apply.
.TP
.B org renew\-pin
0 pins replaced and status shown; 64 bad arguments; 65 sudo, invalid pins, no organization or invalid records;
69 unsafe or unreadable host files; 73 unsafe path, input, lock, publication or unhandled file/data failure.
.TP
.B task show
0 record shown; 64 bad arguments or fingerprint; 65 caller, records or visibility refused;
69 not root or file/account lookup failure; 73 unsafe task path, lock or file failure.
.TP
.B task sweep
0 sweep complete; 64 bad arguments; 65 sudo or invalid records;
69 not root, dependency, revocation or forge failure; 73 unsafe task path, lock or file failure.
.TP
.B task start
0 task recorded and token answered, if enabled; 64 bad arguments, input form or no SUDO_USER;
65 caller, certificate or task conflict; 69 not root, file, dependency or forge failure;
73 unsafe task path, recording, lock or file failure.
.TP
.B task end
0 token ended; 64 bad arguments, task name or no SUDO_USER; 65 caller, records or unknown task;
69 not root, file, dependency or forge failure; 73 unsafe task path, lock or file failure.
.TP
.B forge issue\-token, forge lend\-token
0 token answered; 64 bad arguments or no SUDO_USER; 65 caller or records refused;
69 not root, no forge, file, dependency or forge failure; 73 lock or unhandled file failure.
.TP
.B forge gate
0 connection served, including HTTP refusals; 1 unusable activation or connection failure;
64 bad arguments or relative upstream; 65 sudo invocation; 69 account lookup failure.
.TP
.B forge measure
0 all requests succeeded; 1 failed request or connection/execution failure;
64 bad arguments or invalid measurement parameters.
.TP
.B pool revert
0 one VM settled; 1 revert, list, watch, file or dependency failure;
2 bad arguments or VM name; 65 sudo invocation.
.TP
.B pool give\-back
0 poweroff scheduled; 1 file or dependency failure;
2 bad arguments, missing/invalid sudo caller or absent run account.
The systemd-run exit status is propagated.
.TP
.B pool make
0 VM imported; 1 invalid name, existing VM, file, dependency or creation failure;
2 bad arguments or invalid VM parameters; 65 sudo invocation.
.TP
.B pool address
0 IPv4 lease shown; 1 bad arguments, invalid name/wait, stopped VM, timeout,
file or dependency failure; 65 sudo invocation.
.TP
.B pool delete
0 domain and volumes absent; 1 bad arguments, invalid name, unreadable census,
remaining domain/volume, file or dependency failure; 65 sudo invocation.
.TP
.B project add
0 registered; 64 bad arguments/signing key or interrupted registration;
65 registration, data, inherited lock or forge refusal;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 file/output write failure.
.TP
.B project remove
0 removed; 64 bad arguments/central token or interrupted push check;
65 project, data, inherited lock, forge refusal or unpushed work;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 file/output write failure.
.TP
.B project migrate
0 migrated; 64 bad arguments/JSON; 65 project, address, key, inherited lock or forge refusal;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 file/output write failure.
.TP
.B project take\-off
0 removed; 64 bad arguments/JSON; 65 project, data, inherited lock, forge refusal or unpushed work;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 file/output write failure.
.TP
.B project central\-state
0 state shown, including cannot-say; 64 bad arguments; 65 project, data or inherited lock refused;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 output write failure.
.TP
.B project give\-back\-check
0 all mains at central; 64 bad arguments; 65 data, wrong lock or main not known to be at central;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 output write failure.
.TP
.B project project\-copy
0 copy or unchanged main; 64 bad arguments; 65 project, address, data or inherited lock refused;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 directory creation or output write failure.
.TP
.B project project\-advance
0 comparison reported; 64 bad arguments; 65 project, changed copy, data or inherited lock refused;
69 not root, dependency/read/lock, Git or cleanup failure; 73 file/output write failure.
.TP
.B project check\-central
0 all commits at central; 64 bad arguments/oversized token; 65 CA path or unpushed work;
66 local token unreadable/empty or unsafe CA; 69 dependency/configuration/read/Git failure;
73 diagnostic write failure; 75 no central token; 77 sudo invocation.
.TP
.B project measure\-central
0 published, possibly empty; 64 bad arguments; 65 project, data or inherited lock refused;
69 not root or local dependency/read/lock failure; 73 publication, ACL or diagnostic write failure.
.TP
.B project pool\-key send
0 key sent or already current; 64 bad arguments; 65 project data, inherited lock or forge refusal;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 marker or output write failure.
.TP
.B project pool\-key withdraw
0 withdrawn, absent or forge gone; 64 bad arguments; 65 data, wrong lock or forge refusal;
69 not root or dependency/read/lock failure; 73 output write failure.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/porelo-host/arrivals/<project>/<repository>.jsonl
Root's private append/fsync ledger of pre-receive judgements, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /etc/porelo-host/organization/arrivals/<project>/<repository>.jsonl
Complete UTF-8 JSON Lines snapshot, atomically replaced after a judged push.
Root owns the file; named-user read ACLs grant only the applied project's unlocked
members with an unlocked, non-pending person record and an existing local account.
Both the applied organization and person file must carry an unrevoked, unexpired card.
The file starts at 0600, with a read-only ACL mask; others cannot read it.
Publication directories are 0711. Follow revokes old readers before slow work,
republishes existing ledgers, and prunes departed projects and repositories.
Repository and project removal revoke publications before deleting private history.
Rows carry verdict and arrived; accepted rows add commit, ref and signer,
migrated rows name pre-judge commits, and armed marks hook installation.
This records the judgement, not proof of a later forge ref update.
Porelod reads this snapshot directly through Tasks1 Arrivals, checking root ownership,
regular-file type, all parents, no symlinks and no group/other write permissions.
Missing or refused data answers cannot-say; no sudo reading verb exists.
Publication failure refuses the push and removes the stale public answer.
Publication streams the complete ledger; the daemon's 16 MiB read bound never
prevents further judgements or follow. Follow scans the locked private ledger with
a 128 KiB bound per line. Arming records migration history privately, and follow
publishes it after admitting members. Empty files are unavailable; an empty armed
repository has an armed row. The daemon accepts snapshots up to 16 MiB and folds
repository display casing to lowercase. Task MCP reads stay in the task's project.
A failed revocation keeps publication closed until
root repairs its cause and successfully runs porelo-host org follow.
.TP
.B /etc/porelo-host/
Root's forge.conf, people records and organization state, pin and project progress.
.TP
.B /var/lib/porelo-host/tasks/<login>/<task>.json
Root's private, durable task certificate records.
.TP
.B /usr/sbin/porelo-host
The host program.
.TP
.B /proc/self/status
Kernel source of the real uid.
.TP
.B /proc/self/uid_map
Namespace mapping used for root-owned file and program checks.
.TP
.B /proc/sys/kernel/overflowuid
Owner uid reported for an unmapped host root.
.TP
.B /etc/nsswitch.conf
The system account database configuration used by getent passwd.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR porelo (1),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR systemd (1),
.BR getent (1)
.SH PROJECT COMMANDS
.TP
.B project add <project> <admin> \-\-signed-by <key>
Register a project named for this admin in the applied organization.
The canonical Ed25519 signing key is pinned in root's private record.
The organization gets its teams and an isolated package publisher.
.TP
.B project remove <project>
Check every repository ref against central before deleting this project's
repositories, packages, organization, publisher, memberships and publications.
A partial removal keeps its record for retry.
.TP
.B project migrate <project>
Read one JSON object containing address and token from standard input.
Migrate the repository, install a read-only deploy key and protect main.
The token stays in memory and the private forge request.
.TP
.B project take-off <project>
Read one JSON object containing repository and token (or null) from stdin.
Check all work before deleting the repository and its read key.
Without a token, report the central deploy key for its owner to remove.
.TP
.B project central-state <project>
Print the compact JSON central-state record without making an admin token.
Every repository is up-to-date, ahead, moved-on or cannot-say.
.TP
.B project pool-key send|withdraw
Send /var/lib/porelo-host/pool/run-key as the PORELO_POOL_SSH organization
secret to registered projects, or withdraw only that secret.
Send again when the key changes or an organization lacks it; record its SHA-256
in run-key.sent only after every upload succeeds. The key and marker are private.
Print compact JSON containing the sorted changed project names.
Withdrawal succeeds without making a token when forge.conf, the forge account,
or the answering forge is gone. An answering forge's refusal stops withdrawal.
.PP
Every project verb except check-central accepts a trailing \-\-lock-fd <descriptor> for the
follow's existing exclusive flock on the trusted forge.conf open description.
Private records have mode 0600, their directories 0700.
A write failure exits 73; data refusal exits 65; unavailable dependencies exit 69.
.TP
.B project give-back-check
Check every registered project's main before returning the host.
Refuse when any main is ahead, moved-on, or cannot-say; remove nothing.
.TP
.B project project-copy <project> <address> <known-commit|->
Read central main using the repository's read key and pinned SSH host keys.
Remove old copies; return a private bare copy owned by the forge account,
or a null copy when main is unchanged. The enclosing directory has mode 0711.
.TP
.B project project-advance <project> <repository> <commit> <copy> <signed|tip>
Bundle the copy as its owner and advance the forge's main only forward.
The signed reach takes only project.yml changes or an exact Porelo-Signs-Anew
trailer for that file. The tip reach follows unused repositories, except those
holding workflows or naming snippet_file. Tell Forgejo after moving the ref.
Print up-to-date, moved, ahead, diverged, other-changes or moved-unseen as JSON.
A changed copy exits 65; a Git or cleanup failure exits 69.
.TP
.B project check-central <project> <repository>...
Root without sudo only: compare every local ref with central reachability.
Read migrated original_url values over /run/porelo-forge/git.sock and clone
from the token gate on literal 127.0.0.1 at forge.conf's git_port.
Read the central token from stdin without echo and the local token as its owner;
no token enters argv or diagnostics. Refuse unpushed commits with 65.
Use forge/central-ca.conf or the system CA bundle for central only and refuse
redirects. Neither forge is written. This verb takes no inherited lock fd.
.TP
.B project measure-central <project>
Publish the comparison to organization/central/<project>.json with the follow's
project, read_at and repositories fields, spaced JSON and a trailing newline.
Grant read access only to the applied project's unlocked, admitted members
whose local accounts exist. Replace the old file's readers before asking central.
A failed question publishes an empty list, never an up-to-date answer.

.SS org follow
Follow the signed organization from root's timer. Requires root without SUDO_USER.
Fetch and validate the complete state before changing access. Removed readers lose
access before slow service and central questions. Project operations run in this
process under the host configuration lock. The whole follow has five minutes;
project-file reads share sixty seconds and central measurements share 120 seconds.
.SS org refresh
Follow immediately from the window over root's SSH connection. The central token
arrives on standard input alone, at most 4096 bytes, for adding the host's read key
or a waiting migration. Empty input follows without migrating. A token nobody
waits for is refused. The result is the complete organization status as JSON.
.SS org renew-pin
Take one login and canonical SSH key per line on standard input from root on the
machine. Duplicate keys and malformed lines are refused. The pin is replaced
atomically and the saved fetch refusal is cleared; the accepted revision remains.
.SH FORGE AND POOL
.TP
.B forge gate --web|--git|--runner --upstream SOCKET
Serve one accepted connection from descriptor 3, with LISTEN_FDS=1 and
LISTEN_PID equal to this process's pid. The forge account's socket services
may run this verb without root; a sudo invocation is refused.
Web and runner require a Unix socket. Git also accepts loopback TCP.
Web checks the peer's admission, Host and origin before forwarding.
Framed web responses permit another request on the same connection;
refusals and token gates close it. Reads and writes time out after 60 seconds.
.TP
.B forge measure --socket SOCKET --host HOST:PORT
[--requests 1000] [--concurrency 32] [--per-connection 8] [--path /]
measures GET requests through an installed gate, including activation.
Run as an admitted person against the web socket, before and after switching
its service executable, with identical arguments and forge contents.
The JSON reports requests, connections, failures, seconds, requests_per_second,
p50_ms and p95_ms. A non-200 response or failed exchange fails the measure.
This verb also runs without root.
.TP
.B pool revert LIST
The root pool service's lifecycle watch. Each round starts its watch before
the census. A stopped VM is restored paused with its link down, resumed,
and given the host's clock before its link goes up.
.B pool revert --one NAME
settles just one VM for up, retrying a VM left off after a failed clock correction.
Reverts share the flock on /usr/local/libexec/porelo-host with the existing
service, with a five-minute lock bound. Recovery notes live in
/run/porelo-pool-revert; the service clears old off notes on restart.
.TP
.B pool give-back
Mark /var/lib/porelo-pool/SUDO_USER resetting and schedule poweroff two
seconds later through systemd-run. Takes no account argument.
.TP
.B pool make NAME VOLUME SEED MEMORY VCPUS OSINFO
Import a pool VM with a prepared cloud-init seed, images storage pool,
default network, a 20G disk and host-passthrough CPU. The guest-agent channel
is always installed. Seed construction remains the build tool's job.
All child programs use checked paths, fixed environment and no shell.
Only give-back runs through sudo; the other pool verbs are root's alone.
.TP
.B pool address NAME [SECONDS]
Wait for the VM's IPv4 lease, for 300 seconds unless a limit is given.
A stopped VM or an expired wait fails with exit 1.
.TP
.B pool delete NAME
Stop and undefine the VM, delete its disk and seed volume, then check
libvirt's domain and volume lists. Anything left behind is a failure.
.PP
Web admission is resolved once per accepted connection; host, route and
credential checks still run for every request. The measure reserves work
before connecting, so its connection count includes no idle activations.
Pool and measure execution failures exit 1; give-back refusals exit 2.
Gate activation refusals exit 1. Pool diagnostics keep their script's
wording; measurement diagnostics begin with "porelo-host forge measure:".
